WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.231 --> 00:00:03.314
<v Daniel>Imagine you hire like this brilliantly educated intern for your team.</v>

2
00:00:03.574 --> 00:00:05.336
<v Maya>Okay. Yeah. Sounds good so far.</v>

3
00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:13.082
<v Daniel>Right. You give them a desk, a computer, and you tell your staff, you know, hey, this intern is available 24-7 to help you out with literally any problem.</v>

4
00:00:13.563 --> 00:00:17.126
<v Maya>Oh, wow. I mean, you'd expect your team's output to just absolutely skyrocket.</v>

5
00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:26.354
<v Daniel>Exactly. But what if at the end of the month you check the metrics and not only has your team's performance plummeted, but like half of them have essentially stopped showing up to work?</v>

6
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:36.845
<v Maya>I mean, you'd probably assume the intern was like actively sabotaging the company. It completely breaks our mental models for how productivity tools are actually supposed to function.</v>

7
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.363
<v Daniel>And that broken mental model is, well, it's the central paradox we're doing a deep dive on today.</v>

8
00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:43.144
<v Maya>Yeah, it's a big one.</v>

9
00:00:43.824 --> 00:00:51.169
<v Daniel>Because as you are looking deeply into the future of work and higher education in New York City, you've got this glaring contradiction just staring you in the face.</v>

10
00:00:51.189 --> 00:00:51.689
<v Maya>You really do.</v>

11
00:00:52.350 --> 00:01:03.750
<v Daniel>On one hand, artificial intelligence makes early career workers wildly productive on the job. But on the other hand, dropping it into university classrooms seems to be like actively tanking students' grades.</v>

12
00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:04.471
<v Maya>Which is wild.</v>

13
00:01:04.691 --> 00:01:12.297
<v Daniel>It is. We need to figure out why, and we need to pull out hard, slide-ready arguments and evidence for your strategy going forward.</v>

14
00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:19.662
<v Maya>Right. And the absolute foundation for those arguments is going to come from this massive new paper published in October 2026. Oh, yeah.</v>

15
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:20.523
<v Daniel>This one is huge.</v>

16
00:01:20.963 --> 00:01:29.068
<v Maya>It's by Liu and colleagues out of the University of Maryland. And they ran a randomized university trial of course integrated AI tutoring.</v>

17
00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:30.249
<v Daniel>Which is super rare, right?</v>

18
00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:45.041
<v Maya>So rare. The scale and rigor of the study are just unmatched. But the Maryland data presents this deeply confusing puzzle. Yeah, it really does. So to solve it, we're going to layer in four recent background papers from Germany, Chile and Tennessee that map out the hidden</v>

19
00:01:45.061 --> 00:01:48.083
<v Maya>psychology of how humans actually interact with these tools.</v>

20
00:01:48.704 --> 00:01:57.992
<v Daniel>OK, so let's start with the crime scene in Maryland, because I feel like we've all sort of coasted on this utopian assumption that AI tutors would be, you know, the great equalizer.</v>

21
00:01:58.283 --> 00:02:01.484
<v Maya>Oh, totally. Like it was going to democratize higher education overnight.</v>

22
00:02:01.664 --> 00:02:07.686
<v Daniel>Right. But this data paints a much, much darker picture. What did the researchers actually do?</v>

23
00:02:08.206 --> 00:02:15.329
<v Maya>Well, they wanted to mimic what a real university-wide rollout looks like. So in the fall of 2025, they enlisted 30 instructors.</v>

24
00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:15.969
<v Daniel>30?</v>

25
00:02:16.189 --> 00:02:16.369
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

26
00:02:16.649 --> 00:02:17.510
<v Daniel>That's a solid sample.</v>

27
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:27.793
<v Maya>And they were teaching 2,379 undergraduate students. We are talking about everything from biology and computer science to like English and public policy.</v>

28
00:02:28.293 --> 00:02:35.019
<v Daniel>OK, so we can't just wave the results away and say, oh, well, it was only computer science majors who know how to cheat. It was across the board.</v>

29
00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:50.011
<v Maya>Exactly. It's broadly representative of a typical university ecosystem. And they randomized the instructors within 13 blocks of identical or similar courses. Got it. But the absolute cleanest comparison came from nine of those blocks, which were different</v>

30
00:02:50.051 --> 00:02:51.673
<v Maya>sections of the exact same course.</v>

31
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:56.697
<v Daniel>Meaning what? Student A and student B have the same syllabus and exams?</v>

32
00:02:57.052 --> 00:03:00.695
<v Maya>Exactly. Same assignments, same exams, but they're just in different sections.</v>

33
00:03:01.036 --> 00:03:09.303
<v Daniel>Okay. And what kind of AI are we talking about here? Because, I mean, if they just gave them raw chat GPT, we already know it hallucinates fake textbook citations and all that.</v>

34
00:03:09.623 --> 00:03:22.114
<v Maya>Right. And that's a really critical detail. They didn't just give them a raw language model. Whole sections either received a custom GPT 4.0 virtual study assistant built directly into the course website, or they didn't.</v>

35
00:03:22.354 --> 00:03:24.416
<v Daniel>Oh, wow. So it was fully integrated.</v>

36
00:03:25.088 --> 00:03:34.732
<v Maya>Yes, and this tool was strictly ring-fenced. It was configured to draw solely on the instructor's own provided course materials, complete with accurate citations.</v>

37
00:03:35.012 --> 00:03:43.536
<v Daniel>Okay, so an open-book, hyper-specific teaching assistant available at 2 in the morning. I mean, grades must have improved, right? Even just slightly?</v>

38
00:03:43.836 --> 00:03:48.218
<v Maya>Well, before we get to the grades, we actually need to look at how the instructors chose to deploy it.</v>

39
00:03:48.238 --> 00:03:49.018
<v Daniel>Okay, what do you mean?</v>

40
00:03:49.692 --> 00:03:55.977
<v Maya>The system had two settings. Instructors could choose tutor mode, which installs pedagogical guardrails.</v>

41
00:03:56.097 --> 00:03:56.437
<v Daniel>Like what?</v>

42
00:03:57.298 --> 00:04:02.782
<v Maya>Like if a student asks a question, tutor mode answers with a guiding question to prompt critical thinking.</v>

43
00:04:03.022 --> 00:04:03.362
<v Daniel>Oh, nice.</v>

44
00:04:03.603 --> 00:04:11.869
<v Maya>Right. But the other option was direct instruction, which acts more like a traditional search engine. It just gives the student the explanation or the answer outright.</v>

45
00:04:12.609 --> 00:04:17.193
<v Daniel>Oh, I'm going to guess, knowing how pressed for time everyone is, that tutor mode wasn't very popular.</v>

46
00:04:17.444 --> 00:04:23.546
<v Maya>You'd be right. Out of all the participating instructors, only two ended the semester still using tutor mode.</v>

47
00:04:23.686 --> 00:04:24.986
<v Daniel>Wow. Only two?</v>

48
00:04:25.086 --> 00:04:27.967
<v Maya>Yeah. Almost everyone defaulted to direct instruction.</v>

49
00:04:28.488 --> 00:04:37.410
<v Daniel>I mean, it makes sense. Direct instruction is easier. You don't get complaints from students saying, like, the AI is being stubborn and refusing to just give them a definition of a cell wall.</v>

50
00:04:37.931 --> 00:04:38.371
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

51
00:04:39.251 --> 00:04:45.233
<v Daniel>So they have this incredibly capable direct answering study buddy. Let's hear the numbers.</v>

52
00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:56.215
<v Maya>All right, brace yourself. Within sections of the exact same course, having access to this AI lowered final grades by 0.37 standard deviations.</v>

53
00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:57.096
<v Daniel>Wait, lowered?</v>

54
00:04:57.436 --> 00:05:01.938
<v Maya>Yes. And the effects on homework were either completely flat or strictly negative.</v>

55
00:05:02.318 --> 00:05:08.942
<v Daniel>Okay, let's translate that for a second because 0.37 standard deviation sounds kind of small if you're not a statistician.</v>

56
00:05:09.102 --> 00:05:09.262
<v Maya>Right.</v>

57
00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.404
<v Daniel>In a real college classroom, what does that actually mean for a student's transcript?</v>

58
00:05:14.141 --> 00:05:28.905
<v Maya>In higher education, a drop of that magnitude is highly significant. In practical terms, think of it as the difference between a student comfortably securing a B and suddenly sweating over a C minus. Yeah, it is a noticeable transcript</v>

59
00:05:29.045 --> 00:05:29.865
<v Maya>altering decline.</v>

60
00:05:30.425 --> 00:05:36.647
<v Daniel>But I mean, dropping a full letter grade doesn't just happen in a vacuum. What happened to their daily habits? Like, were they still doing the work?</v>

61
00:05:37.247 --> 00:05:39.207
<v Maya>Their daily habits completely evaporated.</v>

62
00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:40.088
<v Daniel>Seriously?</v>

63
00:05:40.188 --> 00:05:52.430
<v Maya>Yeah. The researchers looked at recorded participation on the course site, meaning discussion board posts, quiz attempts, assignment submissions. That metric fell by a staggering 0.90 standard deviations.</v>

64
00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:54.031
<v Daniel>0.90?</v>

65
00:05:54.291 --> 00:05:57.151
<v Maya>That is not just a slight dip. That is a ghost town.</v>

66
00:05:57.311 --> 00:05:57.831
<v Daniel>It really is.</v>

67
00:05:58.572 --> 00:06:07.876
<v Maya>The number of active days students even bothered to log into the course site dropped by 0.51 standard deviations. and their page views fell by 0.37.</v>

68
00:06:08.456 --> 00:06:15.383
<v Daniel>I just, I can't get over that. You gave them a tool to help them study, and their response was to basically stop going to school entirely.</v>

69
00:06:15.982 --> 00:06:30.935
<v Maya>And that drop-off in engagement is really the defining characteristic of the study. And this brings us to a massive flashing warning light for your strategy in New York. Right. Because you have a university system like CUNY serving a massive population of early career workers,</v>

70
00:06:31.355 --> 00:06:33.677
<v Maya>adult learners, and first-generation college students.</v>

71
00:06:33.978 --> 00:06:36.440
<v Daniel>Let me guess. The AI didn't hurt everyone equally, did it?</v>

72
00:06:36.838 --> 00:06:45.891
<v Maya>Not at all. The disparity is actually really alarming. First-generation college students saw their final grades plummet by 0.50 standard deviations. Oh,</v>

73
00:06:45.951 --> 00:06:46.091
<v Daniel>man.</v>

74
00:06:46.452 --> 00:06:49.817
<v Maya>Yeah, and for students who were not first-generation, the drop was only 0.22.</v>

75
00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:59.217
<v Daniel>So the academic harm was disproportionately concentrated on the exact demographic that institutions rely on AI to help. That's devastating.</v>

76
00:06:59.477 --> 00:06:59.737
<v Maya>It is.</v>

77
00:07:00.498 --> 00:07:08.425
<v Daniel>So I'm trying to visualize like a student sitting in their dorm room using this thing. Are they even trying to learn? What are they actually typing into the prompt box?</v>

78
00:07:08.745 --> 00:07:11.427
<v Maya>We actually know. The server logs give us the exact breakdown.</v>

79
00:07:11.508 --> 00:07:11.948
<v Daniel>Oh, perfect.</v>

80
00:07:12.313 --> 00:07:22.503
<v Maya>About 74% of all student requests asked the AI for information, explanations, or direct solutions. Another 11% wanted practice questions or test prep.</v>

81
00:07:23.123 --> 00:07:29.549
<v Daniel>And what about, like, asking the AI to review a draft of their essay or maybe check their math logic?</v>

82
00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:34.694
<v Maya>Less than 1% of requests asked the AI for feedback on the student's own original work.</v>

83
00:07:35.081 --> 00:07:35.922
<v Daniel>Less than 1%.</v>

84
00:07:36.182 --> 00:07:36.382
<v Maya>Yep.</v>

85
00:07:37.163 --> 00:07:42.066
<v Daniel>So nobody wants the robot to critique their thinking. They just want the robot to deliver the finished product.</v>

86
00:07:42.266 --> 00:07:48.711
<v Maya>Exactly. Which is, you know, it's human nature. But that human nature creates the most baffling puzzle in the entire Maryland trial.</v>

87
00:07:49.232 --> 00:07:51.153
<v Daniel>Okay, I'm intrigued. What's the puzzle?</v>

88
00:07:51.733 --> 00:08:01.961
<v Maya>Well, if the grades fell by a mass of 0.37 standard deviations and participation dropped off a cliff, you'd naturally assume the students were constantly using the AI and getting fed bad answers, right?</v>

89
00:08:02.141 --> 00:08:02.802
<v Daniel>Yeah, that would make sense.</v>

90
00:08:03.445 --> 00:08:11.929
<v Maya>But out of all the students in the treated sections, the ones who actually had the AI button on their screen, only about 15% ever actually clicked it and used it.</v>

91
00:08:12.069 --> 00:08:12.549
<v Daniel>Wait, what?</v>

92
00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.812
<v Maya>And those 15% only averaged about four sessions over the entire semester.</v>

93
00:08:18.452 --> 00:08:26.136
<v Daniel>Hold on. Let me make sure I'm following this. Only 15% of the class used the tool, but the grades for the entire section tanked.</v>

94
00:08:26.656 --> 00:08:32.719
<v Maya>Yes. The grades fell about as much for the non-users in the treated sections as they did for the active users.</v>

95
00:08:33.678 --> 00:08:41.744
<v Daniel>How is that even logically possible? If a student never clicks the AI button, how does the mere existence of the button lower their final exam score?</v>

96
00:08:42.345 --> 00:08:49.711
<v Maya>We have to look at the psychology of the classroom environment for this one. The author's leading explanation is a phenomenon called a norm shift.</v>

97
00:08:50.191 --> 00:08:50.912
<v Daniel>A norm shift.</v>

98
00:08:51.232 --> 00:09:02.901
<v Maya>Right. By taking this AI tool and formally integrating it into the course architecture, putting it on the syllabus, embedding it in the university website, the institution sent an implicit psychological signal.</v>

99
00:09:03.843 --> 00:09:04.703
<v Daniel>And what was the signal?</v>

100
00:09:05.544 --> 00:09:09.045
<v Maya>The signal was, cognitive offloading is now sanctioned here.</v>

101
00:09:09.985 --> 00:09:16.087
<v Daniel>Oh. So just by seeing the tool, the students assumed the professor was totally fine with him taking shortcuts.</v>

102
00:09:16.587 --> 00:09:17.047
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

103
00:09:18.068 --> 00:09:25.530
<v Daniel>And instead of using the clunky university AI that logs their prompts, they probably just opened up a private tab of chat GPT and let it do the heavy lifting.</v>

104
00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:35.348
<v Maya>The survey data strongly supports that theory. In the treated sections, 41% of the students reported that their instructor encouraged or embedded generative AI.</v>

105
00:09:35.528 --> 00:09:36.408
<v Daniel>And in the control group?</v>

106
00:09:36.889 --> 00:09:44.135
<v Maya>Taking the exact same course without the integrated tool, only 11% felt that way. The tool literally changed the culture of the room.</v>

107
00:09:44.495 --> 00:09:51.601
<v Daniel>Wow. Now, I know we have to be careful with survey data. What are the caveats here? Because we can't just treat that 41% as absolute gospel.</v>

108
00:09:53.608 --> 00:10:02.875
<v Maya>Oh, for sure. The authors are very honest about the limitations, and you should be too when you build your arguments. First, the response rate to that specific survey was only 18%. Okay,</v>

109
00:10:03.476 --> 00:10:04.096
<v Daniel>that's pretty low.</v>

110
00:10:04.517 --> 00:10:16.086
<v Maya>Second, everyone had free access to ChatGPT and Gemini anyway. Plus, the students knew their instructors could potentially see their prompts in the course-integrated tool, which likely created a chilling effect on legitimate usage.</v>

111
00:10:16.306 --> 00:10:18.267
<v Daniel>Yeah, nobody wants their professor reading their prompts.</v>

112
00:10:18.688 --> 00:10:29.582
<v Maya>Exactly. It pushed them toward their own private accounts. Third, the statistical validity of that norm shift theory rests on about 34 instructor clusters.</v>

113
00:10:29.622 --> 00:10:35.505
<v Daniel>34 clusters doesn't sound like a lot when you're trying to prove a massive university-wide cultural shift.</v>

114
00:10:35.945 --> 00:10:46.071
<v Maya>It isn't, which is why the researchers used a technique called randomization inference. It's essentially a mathematical stress test to prove those 34 clusters weren't just a statistical fluke.</v>

115
00:10:46.452 --> 00:10:46.952
<v Daniel>And it held up.</v>

116
00:10:47.456 --> 00:10:58.700
<v Maya>It held up. But the most vital caveat to remember here is that this disaster happened with this specific tool configured mostly for direct instruction, not well-scaffolded tutoring.</v>

117
00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:11.785
<v Daniel>OK, so if cognitive offloading, just having an AI spit out the answer, is so destructive in a college classroom, why is the corporate world currently pouring billions of dollars into these exact same tools?</v>

118
00:11:12.285 --> 00:11:13.546
<v Maya>That is the big question.</v>

119
00:11:13.566 --> 00:11:16.147
<v Daniel>Right? Like, we need to look at the other side of the paradox.</v>

120
00:11:16.792 --> 00:11:23.377
<v Maya>And for that, we turn to a 2026 paper by Brosh and colleagues. They moved out of the university and into the workplace.</v>

121
00:11:23.557 --> 00:11:24.278
<v Daniel>OK, what did they find?</v>

122
00:11:24.778 --> 00:11:29.762
<v Maya>They tracked 673 final year IT apprentices across 12 German vocational schools.</v>

123
00:11:30.002 --> 00:11:31.283
<v Daniel>So early career workers.</v>

124
00:11:31.624 --> 00:11:38.049
<v Maya>Exactly. Right on the edge of entering the labor market. They randomly gave them access to AI to perform actual job tasks.</v>

125
00:11:38.329 --> 00:11:40.691
<v Daniel>Let me guess. Their performance skyrocketed.</v>

126
00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:56.198
<v Maya>It did. AI raised their task performance substantially without lowering their immediate comprehension. And the gains were even larger for those who already had higher AI literacy. Wow. But the truly remarkable data point emerged when they asked these apprentices to perform tasks that</v>

127
00:11:56.238 --> 00:11:59.060
<v Maya>were explicitly beyond their formal training.</v>

128
00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.061
<v Daniel>Like things they hadn't been taught how to do yet.</v>

129
00:12:01.181 --> 00:12:06.844
<v Maya>Yes. On those stretch tasks, AI access boosted their performance by 26 to 31 percentage points.</v>

130
00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:14.812
<v Daniel>A 30-point jump on a task you don't even know how to do. That is literally a superpower.</v>

131
00:12:14.992 --> 00:12:15.452
<v Maya>It really is.</v>

132
00:12:16.212 --> 00:12:28.755
<v Daniel>But, I mean, it brings us right back to our central contradiction. How can AI make a 21-year-old worker in Germany 30 points better but make a 19-year-old student in Maryland a full letter grade worse?</v>

133
00:12:29.335 --> 00:12:37.237
<v Maya>Because we are fundamentally confusing two entirely different human activities, doing a job and learning a skill.</v>

134
00:12:38.035 --> 00:12:39.115
<v Daniel>Okay, unpack that for me.</v>

135
00:12:39.875 --> 00:12:47.057
<v Maya>AI that raises performance on the job and AI that lowers learning in a course can completely coexist. They are both true.</v>

136
00:12:47.497 --> 00:12:47.677
<v Daniel>How?</v>

137
00:12:48.497 --> 00:13:01.900
<v Maya>The Maryland trial measured learning, which physically requires the brain to undergo internal cognitive struggle to build new neural pathways. But the Brosh trial in Germany measured doing the job, which solely requires external output.</v>

138
00:13:02.603 --> 00:13:08.088
<v Daniel>I absolutely love this distinction. It's exactly like using a GPS app on your phone, isn't it?</v>

139
00:13:08.148 --> 00:13:09.850
<v Maya>Yes, that is the perfect analogy.</v>

140
00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:20.745
<v Daniel>Like, if your only goal is to deliver a pizza as fast as possible, that's the German workplace study, the customer gets the pizza, the output is achieved, and the GPS is a miracle tool.</v>

141
00:13:21.105 --> 00:13:21.325
<v Maya>Right.</v>

142
00:13:21.805 --> 00:13:35.512
<v Daniel>But it is an absolute disaster if your goal is to train for a test on how to navigate the city streets from memory. That's the Maryland University study. If you just blindly follow the blue line on your phone, your brain never builds the map.</v>

143
00:13:35.697 --> 00:13:44.825
<v Maya>Exactly. The cognitive offloading that makes a worker incredibly fast and productive is the exact same mechanism that robs a student of the friction they need to learn.</v>

144
00:13:45.585 --> 00:13:52.011
<v Daniel>So what's the solution for a university then? Do we just ban it entirely because that seems completely impossible?</v>

145
00:13:52.592 --> 00:14:07.544
<v Maya>Banning it is neither practical nor helpful. We definitely can't conclude that AI tutoring doesn't work just because Marilyn implemented it poorly. The defensible claim you can make is this. Dropping a general assistant into a course without guidance or structure displaces the</v>

146
00:14:07.584 --> 00:14:09.305
<v Maya>engagement that actually produces learning.</v>

147
00:14:10.186 --> 00:14:16.411
<v Daniel>Okay. So to see what actually works, we need to look at attempts to force the AI to act like a strict coach.</v>

148
00:14:16.848 --> 00:14:20.930
<v Maya>Which means we need to look at younger students who are even less patient than college kids.</v>

149
00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:22.191
<v Daniel>Oh, middle schoolers.</v>

150
00:14:22.371 --> 00:14:28.854
<v Maya>Yep. A 2026 paper by Oriopoulos and Lowe looked at a two-year trial in 18 Tennessee middle schools.</v>

151
00:14:29.474 --> 00:14:30.575
<v Daniel>Okay. What tool did they use?</v>

152
00:14:31.155 --> 00:14:42.161
<v Maya>They used Khan Academy with Khanmigo, which is an AI tutor heavily guardrailed and configured specifically to coach. It refuses to just give you the bare answers. It asks you questions to guide you there.</v>

153
00:14:42.561 --> 00:14:46.563
<v Daniel>Okay. So they solved the direct instruction problem. Did the test scores blow the roof off?</v>

154
00:14:46.997 --> 00:14:54.422
<v Maya>Not at all. The gains were incredibly modest, about 0.06 to 0.08 standard deviations over a year.</v>

155
00:14:55.003 --> 00:14:55.303
<v Daniel>Really?</v>

156
00:14:55.543 --> 00:15:00.787
<v Maya>Yes. Statistically, that is basically the exact same benefit as using regular Khan Academy without the AI.</v>

157
00:15:01.187 --> 00:15:07.792
<v Daniel>I am stuck on this. If the AI is acting like a perfect patient coach, why didn't the kids learn more?</v>

158
00:15:08.593 --> 00:15:23.518
<v Maya>Because of the engagement constraint. You can design the perfect AI tutor, but you cannot force a human to talk to it. Oh, right. 96% of the students tried the tutor, but the median student only bothered to message it on a third of their practice days.</v>

159
00:15:24.018 --> 00:15:24.659
<v Daniel>That's pretty low.</v>

160
00:15:25.279 --> 00:15:31.761
<v Maya>And more tellingly, in the sessions where they actually made a mistake on a problem, they only engaged the AI 17% of the time.</v>

161
00:15:32.101 --> 00:15:34.042
<v Daniel>They just ignore the tutor entirely when they got stuck.</v>

162
00:15:34.328 --> 00:15:41.732
<v Maya>Or they tried to brute force it. When they did message the tutor, they usually just typed in bare numbers, hoping the AI would validate their guess.</v>

163
00:15:42.152 --> 00:15:43.813
<v Daniel>So they actively avoided the coaching.</v>

164
00:15:44.154 --> 00:15:47.836
<v Maya>Exactly. They didn't want to coach. They wanted the pizza delivered.</v>

165
00:15:47.856 --> 00:15:57.141
<v Daniel>Wow. So if a hands-off approach fails and a strict tutor approach just gets ignored, what is the actual mechanism that builds the cognitive map?</v>

166
00:15:57.503 --> 00:16:04.645
<v Maya>Structure. Another 2026 paper by Oriopoulos and colleagues looked at over 6000 middle school math students.</v>

167
00:16:04.925 --> 00:16:06.005
<v Daniel>OK. Bigger sample.</v>

168
00:16:06.205 --> 00:16:20.448
<v Maya>Yeah. And they found that introducing AI actually made the students slower at first, though it did help them recover from errors. But the critical finding, the only time they saw evidence of delayed learning was when the AI was embedded in a mastery workflow.</v>

169
00:16:20.708 --> 00:16:24.269
<v Daniel>OK. Delayed learning, meaning they actually remembered the math a week later.</v>

170
00:16:24.649 --> 00:16:25.069
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

171
00:16:25.962 --> 00:16:29.004
<v Daniel>So what does a mastery workflow actually look like in practice?</v>

172
00:16:29.345 --> 00:16:37.191
<v Maya>It means the software does not allow the student to move on to the next concept until they have proven they can solve the current problem without the AI's help.</v>

173
00:16:37.491 --> 00:16:37.931
<v Daniel>Oh, I see.</v>

174
00:16:38.272 --> 00:16:46.098
<v Maya>The structure of the assignment forces the friction. The AI is there to unstick them, but the workflow demands they do the heavy lifting themselves eventually.</v>

175
00:16:46.398 --> 00:16:52.943
<v Daniel>So structure is what decides whether AI turns a mistake into learning or just acts as a bypass valve for struggle.</v>

176
00:16:53.074 --> 00:16:58.460
<v Maya>Precisely. You can't just hand someone a sophisticated tool and expect them to use it for personal growth.</v>

177
00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:04.907
<v Daniel>That makes total sense. Which brings us to the final piece of our puzzle, right? A selective university in Chile. Yes.</v>

178
00:17:05.588 --> 00:17:10.894
<v Maya>This is a 2026 study by Gallegos looking at an undergraduate econometrics course.</v>

179
00:17:11.334 --> 00:17:13.697
<v Daniel>Oh, econometrics is notoriously difficult.</v>

180
00:17:14.427 --> 00:17:15.727
<v Maya>heavy math and logic.</v>

181
00:17:16.148 --> 00:17:16.488
<v Daniel>Very.</v>

182
00:17:16.768 --> 00:17:26.091
<v Maya>So the researchers ran two pre-registered interventions with about 300 students. First, they simply encouraged the students to adopt a course-specific AI assistant.</v>

183
00:17:26.611 --> 00:17:30.692
<v Daniel>Let me guess. Usage went up, but midterm scores stayed flat.</v>

184
00:17:30.812 --> 00:17:34.914
<v Maya>Correct. Adoption alone did absolutely nothing for their midterm scores.</v>

185
00:17:35.214 --> 00:17:36.174
<v Daniel>Unsurprising at this point.</v>

186
00:17:37.035 --> 00:17:45.299
<v Maya>So they implemented a second intervention. This time, they didn't just provide the tool. They provided actual intensive guidance on how to use it to learn.</v>

187
00:17:45.459 --> 00:17:46.299
<v Daniel>Like a tutorial?</v>

188
00:17:46.559 --> 00:17:54.903
<v Maya>Yeah. They taught the students how to prompt the AI for explanations, how to verify its logic, and how to use it as a sparring partner rather than an answer key.</v>

189
00:17:55.183 --> 00:17:59.645
<v Daniel>That sounds incredibly resource-intensive for the faculty. Did it actually pay off?</v>

190
00:18:00.218 --> 00:18:12.589
<v Maya>Massively. That specific pedagogical guidance raised final exam scores by 0.21 standard deviations, and it increased a student's chance of passing the econometrics course by 12 percentage points.</v>

191
00:18:13.109 --> 00:18:20.355
<v Daniel>12 points. So the takeaway for universities isn't about AI adoption at all. Adoption is totally irrelevant.</v>

192
00:18:20.616 --> 00:18:20.836
<v Maya>Right.</v>

193
00:18:21.236 --> 00:18:24.699
<v Daniel>Guidance and structure are the only levers that actually move the needle on learning.</v>

194
00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.833
<v Maya>That is the ultimate synthesis of all this data.</v>

195
00:18:28.073 --> 00:18:42.867
<v Daniel>Okay. So let's pull all of this together. You are looking at the landscape of New York City. You have employers demanding these highly productive AI literate workers, and you have institutions like CUNY trying to educate this massive population of first generation and</v>

196
00:18:43.287 --> 00:18:46.791
<v Daniel>adult learners who need reskilling. What are the institutional stakes here?</v>

197
00:18:47.402 --> 00:18:57.686
<v Maya>The stakes are literally the fundamental architecture of a university degree. Faculty and administration can no longer just casually slap an AI-permitted clause on their syllabi.</v>

198
00:18:57.767 --> 00:18:59.447
<v Daniel>Right, because of what happened in Maryland.</v>

199
00:18:59.747 --> 00:19:14.354
<v Maya>Exactly. Maryland ran this trial precisely to decide whether to rule out course-integrated AI campus-wide, and we saw the disastrous results. Institutions must actively configure the tools, build mastery workflows, and teach the meta skill of using AI.</v>

200
00:19:14.954 --> 00:19:16.995
<v Daniel>And like there's an assessment crisis buried in here, too.</v>

201
00:19:17.537 --> 00:19:18.077
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely.</v>

202
00:19:18.438 --> 00:19:29.166
<v Daniel>If the German IT study shows us that AI can flawlessly execute tasks beyond a worker's training, how does a university professor even know if a student has learned anything from a homework assignment?</v>

203
00:19:29.426 --> 00:19:39.994
<v Maya>They don't. Universities are currently built to assess learning by grading external output, essays, problem sets, code. But AI has severed the link between external output and internal learning.</v>

204
00:19:40.455 --> 00:19:44.838
<v Daniel>So institutions have to completely rethink how they measure a student's cognitive map.</v>

205
00:19:45.334 --> 00:19:56.344
<v Maya>They do. And we also cannot ignore the equity inference here. If you are designing programs for a system with heavy first-generation enrollment, the Maryland study is a blaring siren.</v>

206
00:19:56.524 --> 00:19:57.745
<v Daniel>Because it hurt them the most.</v>

207
00:19:58.306 --> 00:20:06.253
<v Maya>It is the most vital warning in the data. Unstructured AI drove a 0.50 standard deviation drop in their grades.</v>

208
00:20:06.453 --> 00:20:07.214
<v Daniel>It is just awful.</v>

209
00:20:07.832 --> 00:20:19.238
<v Maya>The students who already understand how to play the game of academia will use AI to accelerate. But the students who most need institutional support will implicitly take the signal to offload the cognitive friction they desperately need.</v>

210
00:20:19.398 --> 00:20:19.678
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

211
00:20:20.098 --> 00:20:23.740
<v Maya>Without deliberate design, AI will simply widen the educational divide.</v>

212
00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:36.006
<v Daniel>All right. So if I'm summarizing the core arguments, say for a slide deck, I need the three most undeniable findings and I need to know the strongest counter arguments someone might throw back at me. Let's hit the Maryland data first.</v>

213
00:20:36.803 --> 00:20:51.625
<v Maya>The core finding there, in a massive randomized university trial, dropping a course-integrated AI assistant into a class lowered final grades by 0.37 standard deviations and caused a 0.90 standard deviation collapse in</v>

214
00:20:51.685 --> 00:20:52.647
<v Maya>student participation.</v>

215
00:20:53.549 --> 00:21:01.895
<v Daniel>And the strongest objection you'll hear to that is the usage stat, right? Someone will point out that only 15% of the treated students actually clicked the tool.</v>

216
00:21:02.556 --> 00:21:02.996
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

217
00:21:03.336 --> 00:21:15.205
<v Daniel>And you have to counter that by explaining the norm shift. The mere presence of the institutional AI signaled that cognitive offloading was acceptable, likely driving students to use their own unmonitored chat GPT tabs.</v>

218
00:21:15.908 --> 00:21:30.796
<v Maya>Spot on. The second core finding comes from the Chilean econometric study. Adoption is not the lever. Guidance is. Right. Merely adopting an AI tool did nothing but actively guiding students on how to use it for learning, raised final exam scores by</v>

219
00:21:30.816 --> 00:21:35.058
<v Maya>0.21 standard deviations, and boosted passing rates by 12 percentage points.</v>

220
00:21:35.511 --> 00:21:42.775
<v Daniel>But the pushback there is going to be scalability. A critic will correctly point out that this was a single, highly selective econometrics course.</v>

221
00:21:43.276 --> 00:21:43.556
<v Maya>True.</v>

222
00:21:44.016 --> 00:21:53.002
<v Daniel>Scaling that intense level of pedagogical guidance across thousands of diverse classes, from poetry to physics, requires a massive overhaul of how faculty teach.</v>

223
00:21:53.842 --> 00:22:08.788
<v Maya>It's incredibly difficult. And the final core finding bridges the gap to the workforce, from the German IT study. On the job, AI drastically boosts performance. It raised the output of early career workers on stretched tasks by up to 31 percentage</v>

224
00:22:08.848 --> 00:22:09.128
<v Maya>points.</v>

225
00:22:09.828 --> 00:22:21.454
<v Daniel>And the necessary caveat there is the huge difference between performing a task and learning a skill. Just because a worker can output a piece of code using AI doesn't mean a university should give them an A in computer science.</v>

226
00:22:22.094 --> 00:22:26.116
<v Maya>Which leaves universities walking an incredibly precarious tightrope right now.</v>

227
00:22:26.536 --> 00:22:32.519
<v Daniel>It really does. I mean, we started this assuming AI in the classroom was just the modern equivalent of handing every kid a calculator.</v>

228
00:22:32.754 --> 00:22:33.574
<v Maya>Yeah, that was a dream.</v>

229
00:22:34.135 --> 00:22:48.781
<v Daniel>But it is much closer to hiring that brilliant intern. If the intern does all the heavy lifting, the intern gets stronger and you atrophy. So I want to leave you with this final provocative thought as you map out the future of these institutions.</v>

230
00:22:49.061 --> 00:22:49.481
<v Maya>Let's hear it.</v>

231
00:22:50.762 --> 00:23:05.578
<v Daniel>If AI fundamentally separates the act of doing a task from the act of learning a skill, will the university of the future have to abandon the factory model of grading homework? and instead look much more like a cognitive gym, where the</v>

232
00:23:05.618 --> 00:23:10.136
<v Daniel>struggle, the friction, and the sweat are the entire point of showing up.</v>
